        "en": {
          "why_it_matters": "Live from Emmet's Place became compelling because Cohen used the piano less as a showcase than as a meeting point between generations: stride, bebop, blues and modern rhythm could all enter the same room depending on who walked in. Moving that idea into The Appel Room keeps the useful uncertainty intact—an expert trio, veteran guests and younger voices responding in real time rather than a fixed repertory package.",
